\section{Agreement with SAS PROC COMPARE}{

The package tests compare the results with SAS 9.4 \code{PROC COMPARE}
(\code{METHOD=ABSOLUTE}, \code{CRITERION} equal to \code{tolerance}, \code{by} as the
\code{ID} statement) on nine small comparisons typed into
\file{inst/sas/benchmark_proc_compare.sas}; its listing is committed under
\file{inst/sas/list/}. On that data both agree on the number of variables in common and
only in either data set; of observations matched and only in either data set (by key,
by position, and with repeated keys paired in order); of values that differ, per variable
and in all, and of observations with a difference; of values that are not exactly equal
(\code{tolerance = 0}); on the maximum difference; and on every cell that differs. A value
differs if \code{abs(difference) > tolerance} (a difference of exactly \code{tolerance} is
none), and a missing value against a value is a difference. The agreement is on the
variables SAS compares (see the second item below) and on that data; it is not a proof
for other inputs.

The two define some things differently:
\itemize{
  \item The sign: \code{diff} in \code{diffs} is base minus compare; \code{PROC COMPARE}
    prints \code{Diff.} as compare minus base.
  \item A variable whose type differs between the data sets is still compared, as text,
    and reported with \code{types_match = FALSE}; \code{PROC COMPARE} compares nothing
    and counts a conflicting type.
  \item \dQuote{Type} is the R class: \code{integer} against \code{numeric}, or
    \code{factor} against \code{character}, is a type mismatch, although SAS has only
    numeric and character variables.
  \item Strings are compared as they are: a trailing blank is a difference (SAS pads the
    shorter value), and \code{NA} differs from \code{""} (SAS's missing character value
    is the blank).
  \item Only the type is checked, not the length, format, informat or label (SAS reports
    a character variable of different lengths as a differing attribute).
  \item Only absolute differences. \code{METHOD=PERCENT} and \code{RELATIVE}, \code{BY}
    group reports and special missing values are not part of the benchmark.
  \item \code{cbe_compare_df()} needs no sorted data; \code{PROC COMPARE} does (the
    benchmark sorts the COMPARE data set).
}
}

